WEBVTT

NOTE Internet Monitor tour: complaints (en). Generated by tools/tutorial/build.cjs.

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.061
The Complaints page helps you
complain to your provider with proof,

2
00:00:04.061 --> 00:00:06.868
and follow the complaint
until your line is fixed.

3
00:00:06.868 --> 00:00:09.900
Let's walk through one
complaint from start to finish.

4
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:15.335
First, fill in your internet line once:
your country, your provider, the plan and

5
00:00:15.335 --> 00:00:20.122
the speed it promises, your monthly price,
and your line and account numbers.

6
00:00:20.122 --> 00:00:25.033
Then press Save line details.
They go at the top of every report and complaint.

7
00:00:25.433 --> 00:00:29.074
Your line in the last 7 days
sums up what the app measured:

8
00:00:29.074 --> 00:00:33.208
outages, downtime, the longest outage,
uptime, how much of the time

9
00:00:33.208 --> 00:00:37.033
was monitored, and your typical
speed compared with your plan.

10
00:00:37.433 --> 00:00:41.717
First call your provider, or use their app,
and file the complaint there.

11
00:00:41.717 --> 00:00:45.767
Write down the number they give you.
Then press Log a complaint here.

12
00:00:46.167 --> 00:00:50.684
The form opens with a summary of the
last 7 days already in the notes.

13
00:00:50.684 --> 00:00:53.395
Type the complaint number, check the date,

14
00:00:53.395 --> 00:00:56.622
choose how you filed it,
and add the agent's name.

15
00:00:56.622 --> 00:00:58.300
Then press Save complaint.

16
00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:02.448
Your complaint is now in the list,
waiting for the provider.

17
00:01:02.448 --> 00:01:07.633
The app counts down the time the provider
has to respond. In Egypt, that is 3 days.

18
00:01:08.033 --> 00:01:10.384
Days pass, and the app keeps measuring.

19
00:01:10.384 --> 00:01:14.905
Each complaint shows whether the line
is still dropping since you filed it.

20
00:01:14.905 --> 00:01:16.833
This one is: three more outages.

21
00:01:17.233 --> 00:01:19.295
Open Evidence and complaint text.

22
00:01:19.295 --> 00:01:23.043
It compares the 7 days before
you filed with the days since,

23
00:01:23.043 --> 00:01:26.167
so you can show the provider
nothing has improved.

24
00:01:26.567 --> 00:01:31.916
Below is a ready-to-send complaint,
written from your own measurements. You can edit it.

25
00:01:31.916 --> 00:01:36.900
Copy text puts it on the clipboard.
WhatsApp and Email open a new message with it.

26
00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:42.378
Download evidence saves a ZIP with the
report, the raw logs, and a SHA-256

27
00:01:42.378 --> 00:01:47.182
checksum for every file, so anyone
can check that nothing was changed.

28
00:01:47.182 --> 00:01:48.967
Attach it to your message.

29
00:01:49.367 --> 00:01:52.105
When the provider's time
runs out with no fix,

30
00:01:52.105 --> 00:01:56.033
the complaint tells you so,
and you can escalate to the regulator.

31
00:01:56.433 --> 00:01:59.968
How to escalate lists the
steps for your country.

32
00:01:59.968 --> 00:02:04.801
In Egypt, that is the NTRA: call 155,
or use the My NTRA app or its

33
00:02:04.801 --> 00:02:09.633
website, with your line number and
the provider's complaint number.

34
00:02:10.033 --> 00:02:12.530
After you escalate, press I escalated it,

35
00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:15.756
and use Edit to add the
regulator's complaint number.

36
00:02:15.756 --> 00:02:18.253
When the line is fixed, press It's fixed.

37
00:02:18.253 --> 00:02:21.967
Every complaint and its outcome
stays in your monthly report.

38
00:02:22.367 --> 00:02:26.413
On the side, What your data says
points out your strongest arguments,

39
00:02:26.413 --> 00:02:30.167
such as the line itself dropping,
or speeds far below your plan.

40
00:02:30.567 --> 00:02:35.244
Open this month's report puts your
complaints next to the month's measurements.

41
00:02:35.244 --> 00:02:38.500
The report has its own video tour.
Thanks for watching!
